  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Verifying Tool Availability**\n\nI\'ve confirmed `yt-dlp` functions as expected, directly returning stream URLs. The next step is to make this process seamless. I\'m currently figuring out the best way to ensure the `yt-dlp` executable is present and accessible. I\'m exploring bundling it within the project or automatically checking for its existence in locations like `bin/yt-dlp` or a temporary directory like `/tmp/yt-dlp` during server startup within `server.ts` or a helper module.\n\n\n**Defining Core Architectural Rules**\n\nI am now solidifying the architecture. I\'m focusing on the core rules regarding no database, and single-user security using `minecraftprahas@gmail.com`. I\'m going to leverage `fs` for local data management. I\'m considering Google OAuth for authentication, likely utilizing NextAuth.js or a similar solution, including email verification to ensure `minecraftprahas@gmail.com`.\n\n\n**Implementing Authorization Options**\n\nI\'ve decided to allow Google ID token verification and session authentication with email checks against an authorized email. I\'ll configure this through an environment variable, defaulting to `minecraftprahas@gmail.com`. This setup provides both testing flexibility and secure authentication.\n\n\n\n**Optimizing Audio Streaming Architecture**\n\nI\'ve determined that proxying audio streams via Express, handling range headers, is the optimal solution. Using `yt-dlp`\'s stdout or direct HTTP streams through a proxy at `/api/stream-audio?id=XYZ` makes for flawless playback, seeking capabilities, and is immune to YouTube\'s CORS and IP issues. My thoughts shifted to implementing an optional `--extractor-args \"youtube:player_client=android,web\"` to avoid bot blocks.\n\n\n**Designing UI Library Structure**\n\nI\'m now detailing the UI and its data structures. I\'ll focus on handling URL input to trigger `yt-dlp` track fetching and saving into a local library. I\'m working through the dashboard, library, and playlist components, including the structure of `data/playlists.json` for persistence, covering tracks, playlists, liked tracks, and recently played items. I\'m considering efficient ways to update this structure.\n\n\n**Designing Audio Player UI**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user interface of the audio player. I\'m building a sticky bottom player bar similar to YouTube Music\'s, including controls for track navigation, playback, volume, and queue management. The design features a scrubber, like/unlike buttons, and a queue drawer for managing upcoming tracks. I\'m focusing on persistent state and seamless audio streaming, incorporating proxy support.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 190216947
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
